[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 3, "caption": "Figure 1: Overview of SciCore-Omics. a , Model architecture of SciCore-Omics. Histological images and spatial gene expression profiles are encoded by modality-specific encoders, and projected into the language-model space. b , Unified tri-modal token representation. Image, gene virtual tokens and text tokens are jointly modelled in a shared decoder-based backbone. c , Representative downstream tasks supported by SciCore-Omics across multiple biological scales.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel schematic labeled **a**, **b**, and **c** describing a unified tri-modal model workflow for histology image, gene expression, and text inputs.\n\nPanel **a** shows input modalities from a biological sample: an **image** branch with a histology tissue patch and zoomed region, a **gene** branch with a gene expression matrix/heatmap, and a **text prompt** reading approximately “Describe the biological state of this sample using the image patch and gene expression profile.” These inputs are passed through separate components labeled **Vision Encoder**, **Gene Encoder**, and **LLM token embedding**, followed by **Resampler** and **Q-former Projector** modules for the image and gene streams.\n\nPanel **b** shows a **Unified Tri-Modal Token Space** containing token sequences for **Vision Tokens**, **Gene Tokens**, and **Text Tokens**. The token examples include image tokens such as `<image>`, `VIS01`, `VIS02`, `VIS03`; gene tokens such as `<genes>`, `GEN01`, `GEN02`, `GEN03`; and text tokens forming a prompt-like sequence such as “Describe the histology …”.\n\nPanel **c** illustrates four downstream tasks: **Task1: Gene Expression Prediction**, showing a histology patch transformed into a predicted expression/bar profile; **Task2: Spatial Domain Recognition**, showing gene/spatial features mapped to colored tissue domains; **Task3: Histopathology Classification**, showing a histology patch passed to a model that outputs text such as “The patch is Breast tissue …”; and **Task4: Sample Analysis**, showing an H&E tissue overview image passed to a model producing descriptive text beginning “The H&E overview shows …”.\n\nNo quantitative findings or experimental results are shown; this is a conceptual architecture and task overview figure."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 4, "caption": "Figure 2: Progressive multimodal alignment of SciCore-Omics. a , Transcriptome-grounded descriptions are generated from spot-level gene expression, marker genes, enriched pathways and tissue metadata. b , Three-stage training strategy comprising image-text pretraining, gene-text alignment and tri-modal alignment, followed by lightweight reinforcement learning. c , Summary of the pretraining corpus and tissue distribution of the curated spatial transcriptomics data. d , Transcriptome-to-language generation performance of models from different training stages on a held-out validation split of the gene-text paired dataset, evaluated using BLEU-4, ROUGE-L and BERTScore F1.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific schematic and results figure about an LLM-based text generation system for gene expression/spatial transcriptomics data.\n\nPanel **a**: Workflow schematic. A **gene expression matrix** with rows labeled Gene 1, Gene 2, Gene 3, ..., Gene n and a blue-to-purple low/high expression heatmap is transformed into biological feature summaries: **top expressed / marker genes** shown as a bar chart for Gene A-D, **pathway enrichment (ssGSEA)** shown as an enrichment curve, and **tissue / metadata** represented with lung, brain, and heart icons. These inputs feed into an **LLM-based text generation** module that produces a narrative report, with example text identifying **breast tissue**, **top 15 genes**, and **top 5 pathways**.\n\nPanel **b**: Three-stage model training/alignment pipeline. **Stage I: Image-Text Pretraining** maps a histology image through a **Vision Encoder** to visual tokens. **Stage II: Gene-Text Alignment** maps a gene-expression-like matrix through a **Gene Encoder** and **Qformer + Project** module to gene tokens. **Stage III: Tri-modal Alignment** combines visual tokens and gene tokens through a **Fusion Bridge** into unified tokens, followed by **RL** and a chatbot/LLM icon. Token types are indicated in a legend: visual tokens, gene tokens, unified tokens.\n\nPanel **c**: Donut chart of dataset composition with center label **Total 151,182 spots**. Tissue/source categories listed include **Brain, Breast, Colon, Lung, Liver, Skin, Other**, with visible proportions around the chart including 19%, 16%, 15%, 14%, 11%, 9%, and 16%.\n\nPanel **d**: Line chart comparing text-generation metrics across **Base**, **Stage II**, and **Stage III**. Metrics are **BLEU-4**, **ROUGE-L**, and **Bert score**. Scores increase across stages, with Stage III approximately BLEU-4 0.791, ROUGE-L 0.606, and Bert score 0.831, indicating improved performance after tri-modal alignment."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Figure 3: Histology-based gene expression prediction with SciCore-Omics. a , Schematic illustration of molecular-level gene expression prediction. H&E image patches are encoded by the SciCoreOmics vision encoder, followed by a regression head to predict spot-level gene expression profiles. b , Performance comparison on 39 normal human heart spatial transcriptomics sections using PCC and MSE. LP, linear probing with a frozen visual encoder; FT, fine-tuning. Error bars indicate s.d. c , Representative measured and predicted spatial expression maps. d , Scatterplots comparing observed and predicted expression values for representative genes, with Pearson correlations shown.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about histology-based gene expression prediction.\n\nPanel **a**: Workflow schematic. A histology image with a highlighted region is cropped into a histology patch, passed into a **SciCore-Omics Vision Encoder**, producing a histology-derived visual representation. This representation is fed to a **Regression head** to predict spot-level gene expression values, shown as a small bar chart with gene labels including **gene A**, **gene B**, **gene C**, **gene D**, and **gene E**.\n\nPanel **b**: Bar chart comparing model performance across methods. Metrics shown are **Average PCC** and **Average MSE**. Methods in the legend include **SciCore-Omics(lp)**, **SciCore-Omics(ft)**, **HisToGene**, **Hist2ST**, **BLEEP**, and **OMICLIP**. SciCore-Omics variants appear among the higher-performing bars for Average PCC.\n\nPanel **c**: Spatial gene expression visualization. Rows are genes **NPPA**, **TTN**, **MYH7**, and **MYL7**. Columns show **H&E Image**, **Ground Truth**, and **Predicted**. The ground-truth and predicted maps show spot-grid overlays colored by gene expression level, with a color scale labeled from low to high.\n\nPanel **d**: Scatter plots comparing observed expression versus predicted expression for genes **NPPA**, **TTN**, **MYH7**, and **MYL7**. Each plot includes a diagonal reference line and Pearson correlation values: **NPPA r = 0.82**, **TTN r = 0.50**, **MYH7 r = 0.61**, **MYL7 r = 0.63**.\n\nBiological source objects visible: H&E-stained histology tissue sections and spatial transcriptomics spots/gene-expression maps. The figure presents a computational pipeline transforming histology image patches into visual embeddings and then into spot-level gene expression predictions."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 8, "caption": "Figure 4: Spot-level spatial domain recognition in the human DLPFC. a , Schematic illustration of spot-level spatial domain recognition. For each spot, SciCore-Omics takes the matched histology image patch and/or gene expression profile as input and generates a description, from which region labels, pathway labels and marker genes are extracted. b , Test-set macro-F1 scores for brain-region label prediction using linear-probing models computed from frozen embeddings generated by SciCoreOmics, Nicheformer, Geneformer, scGPT, scVI and PCA. c , Accuracy of region, pathway and markergene prediction for models after fine-tuning. Bubble size and colour indicate ACC for each structured readout. Grey crosses indicate unsupported input-output settings. d , Spatial projection of manual and predicted brain-region labels in held-out DLPFC sections. e , UMAP visualization of image-only, geneonly and joint image-gene embeddings coloured by Glutamatergic synapse pathway activity.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about a multimodal omics/image model named **SciCore-Omics**.\n\nVisible content:\n\n- **Panel a:** Schematic model interface. Inputs are an **image patch** showing stained tissue morphology and **gene expression** shown as a small bar chart. These feed into **SciCore-Omics**, which produces a natural-language description and structured readout extraction with three output fields:\n  - **region label**\n  - **pathway label**\n  - **marker genes**\n\n- **Panel b:** Bar chart comparing **F1 macro** performance for **Brain region label** prediction. Methods include SciCore-Omics variants:\n  - image embedding\n  - gene embedding\n  - joint embedding  \n  and baselines including **Nicheformer**, **Geneformer**, **ScGPT**, **ScVI**, and **PCA** variants. SciCore-Omics joint embedding appears highest, with image embedding also high.\n\n- **Panel c:** Bubble-matrix accuracy comparison. Rows include **Scicore-Omics**, **LLaVA-Med**, **BioMedGPT**, **CellWhisperer**, and **Cell2sentence**. Columns are grouped by input type:\n  - **Input gene**\n  - **Input image**\n  - **Input gene + image**  \n  Each group evaluates **Region**, **Pathway**, and **Gene**. Bubble color and size encode **ACC** from low to high. SciCore-Omics shows broad performance across gene, image, and combined input settings; other models have limited or missing entries marked by crosses.\n\n- **Panel d:** Spatial tissue/domain visualization. Top image is titled **true domain labels** and bottom image **predicted**. Both show a tissue section overlaid with colored spatial regions. Legend includes labels **L1, L2, L3, L4, L5, L6, WM**. The predicted map broadly recovers several spatial domains but appears noisier than the true labels.\n\n- **Panel e:** Three 2D embedding scatter plots labeled:\n  - **image_emb**\n  - **gene_emb**\n  - **joint_emb**  \n  Points are colored by a low-to-high scalar color scale. The embeddings show different structures, with the joint embedding combining spatial/biological variation patterns from image and gene modalities.\n\nBiological source objects visible/inferred from labels: stained tissue image patches, spatial tissue section, gene-expression profiles, brain-region/domain labels, pathways, and marker genes."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 9, "caption": "Figure 5: Zero-shot histopathology classification. a , Closed-set zero-shot evaluation using datasetspecific class prompts. b , Accuracy on MHIST, BACH, CRC100K and PatchCamelyon compared with general and biomedical VLM baselines; bold and underlining indicate best and second-best results. c , Accuracy distribution across the four benchmarks for each model. Centre values indicate mean accuracy, and error bars indicate s.d. across benchmarks (n = 4). d , Row-normalized BACH confusion matrices for SciCore-Omics and PathGen-LLaVA, with raw counts in parentheses.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled **a-d** about zero-shot pathology image classification using a frozen model named **SciCore-Omics**.\n\nPanel **a** shows a workflow: a breast histology microscopy input image is passed into a prompt asking classification from labels “normal, benign, in situ carcinoma, invasive carcinoma,” then through a frozen SciCore-Omics inference box, producing text outputs such as “features consistent with in situ carcinoma” and “features consistent with invasive carcinoma.”\n\nPanel **b** is a performance table comparing models **GPT-4o, GPT-5, PathGen-LLaVA, LLaVA-Med, Quilt-LLaVA, scicore-omics** across datasets **MHIST, BACH, CRC100K, PatchCamelyon**. The scicore-omics column is highlighted and has top values for MHIST, BACH, and PatchCamelyon, while CRC100K is highest for GPT-4o.\n\nPanel **c** is a boxplot titled **Overall zero-shot pathology classification performance**, with accuracy (%) distributions for the same models. SciCore-Omics has the highest shown mean accuracy, annotated **56.46 ± 15.62**.\n\nPanel **d** contains two BACH confusion matrices comparing **SciCore-Omics** and **PathGen-LLaVA**. Axes are true label vs predicted label with classes **Normal, Benign, In situ, Invasive**. The matrices show row-normalized proportions and counts. SciCore-Omics predicts multiple classes, while PathGen-LLaVA appears heavily biased toward predicting Normal except for Invasive."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 11, "caption": "Figure 6: H&E-based pathology reasoning with SciCore-Omics. a , Fine-tuned PathVQA performance on open-ended and closed-ended questions compared with biomedical VLM baselines. b , H&Eonly case-level reasoning workflow, from low-power slide review to high-power patch assessment and case-level synthesis; Ki-67 immunohistochemistry and pathology reports were used only for expert evaluation. c , Expert assessment of SciCore-Omics, Qwen3-VL and LLaVA-Med on 10 breast cancer cases across six reasoning dimensions. d , Representative proliferation-state inference from tumour-rich regions, high-power morphology and aggregated case-level evidence.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical AI/pathology figure about H&E slide-based tumor assessment and case-level evidence synthesis.\n\nPanel **a**: Table comparing methods with **Open** and **Closed** scores. Methods shown: SciCore-Omics, MMQ, M2I2, BioMedGPT, LLaVA-Med. SciCore-Omics has Open **38.10** and Closed **88.66**; LLaVA-Med has Open **37.95** and Closed **91.21** highlighted.\n\nPanel **b**: Workflow diagram. Input is **H&E only**, shown as a whole-slide histology image. The process includes **low-magnification overview**, **tumor patch selection**, and **high-magnification morphology reasoning**. Some candidate patches are marked with red Xs, while selected histology patches proceed to feature reasoning. Visible morphology labels include **cellularity**, **nuclear atypia**, **hyperchromasia**, **suspected mitoses**, **necrosis**, and **stromal reaction**. Evidence from patches is aggregated into an **evidence summary** with high/low/uncertain indicators, supporting **case-level evidence synthesis**. Pathologist evaluation is shown using an independent reference: **Ki-67 IHC + pathology report**.\n\nPanel **c**: Radar/spider plot comparing **SciCore-Omics**, **qwen3vl**, and **LLaVA-Med** across evaluation dimensions: **Accuracy**, **Balanced accuracy**, **Reasoning completeness**, **Evidence quality**, **Molecular consistency**, and **Case-level synthesis**. SciCore-Omics appears strongest overall, especially on evidence-related and synthesis dimensions.\n\nPanel **d**: Example conversational/model-interface trace for pathology reasoning. It shows user prompts paired with H&E images and assistant responses. The steps include reviewing a low-power H&E overview to identify tumor-rich regions, assessing a selected high-power patch for proliferation-related morphology, and synthesizing multiple representative H&E regions for case-level Ki-67 inference. Red-highlighted text emphasizes findings such as **tumor-stroma interfaces**, **poorly formed glandular regions**, **Ki-67-high tendency**, and **case-level prediction: Ki-67 high**.\n\nBiological source objects: digitized histopathology images from **H&E-stained tissue sections**, plus reference **Ki-67 immunohistochemistry** mentioned in the evaluation workflow.\n\nTransformations/interfaces: whole-slide image input is broken into candidate patches, filtered/selected, analyzed for high-magnification morphology, aggregated into patch-level evidence, and synthesized into a case-level prediction using a model-style dialogue interface.\n\nVisible finding: the method is presented as improving pathology reasoning and case-level Ki-67 inference from H&E images, with quantitative comparisons and qualitative evidence synthesis examples."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 24, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic workflow with three sequential panels connected by right-facing arrows.\n\nPanel labels:\n- Stage I: Image-Text Pretraining\n- Stage II: Gene-Text Alignment\n- Stage III: Tri-modal Alignment\n\nVisible model components:\n- Each stage contains an LLM block at the top.\n- Stage I shows a Vis. Encoder connected through a Resampler to the LLM, with a document/text icon also feeding into the LLM.\n- Stage II shows two parallel input branches: Gen. Encoder to Gen. Adapter to the LLM, and Vis. Encoder to Resampler to the LLM, plus a document/text icon feeding into the LLM.\n- Stage III combines the gene and visual branches: Gen. Encoder + Gen. Adapter, Vis. Encoder + Resampler, and a document/text input, all connected to the LLM.\n\nVisual annotations:\n- Flame icons appear next to components in Stages I and III, indicating trainable or active modules.\n- Snowflake icons appear next to the LLM and visual branch in Stage II, indicating frozen modules.\n- Colored branch backgrounds distinguish gene-related components from visual-related components.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- No explicit biological specimens, cells, tissues, organisms, or molecular structures are shown.\n- The only biological modality indicated is abstract “gene” input represented by the labels Gen. Encoder and Gen. Adapter.\n\nTransformations/interfaces:\n- Image features are processed by a visual encoder and resampler before interfacing with the LLM.\n- Gene features are processed by a gene encoder and gene adapter before interfacing with the LLM.\n- Text/document input is shown as a direct input to the LLM.\n- The figure depicts progressive multimodal alignment from image-text pretraining to gene-text alignment to tri-modal alignment.\n\nFindings:\n- No quantitative results or experimental findings are shown; the figure communicates model training/alignment stages and component freezing/training status."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 24, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 2: Composition of the SciCore-Omics training corpus. Summary of textonly, image-text, gene-text and image-gene-text data used for progressive multimodal alignment. The corpus included approximately 1.05 million images, 39.85 million text sentences and 151,182 tri-modal spatial transcriptomics spots across multiple tissue types.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image contains two donut charts comparing dataset composition.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Left panel titled “Text Sentences”\n  - Center label: “39.85M sentences”\n  - Categories shown by color:\n    - Pretraining: 71.6%, 28.52M\n    - Biomedical / Pathology: 11.5%, 4.58M\n    - Spatial Omics: 9.4%, 3.74M\n    - General-domain: 7.6%, 3.01M\n\n- Right panel titled “Images”\n  - Center label: “1.05M images”\n  - Categories shown by color:\n    - Pretraining: 47.8%, 503K\n    - Biomedical / Pathology: 27.0%, 284K\n    - Spatial Omics: 18.8%, 198K\n    - General-domain: 6.3%, 67K\n\nLegend:\n- Blue: Pretraining\n- Orange: Biomedical / Pathology\n- Green: Spatial Omics\n- Purple: General-domain\n\nBiological source objects:\n- No direct biological specimens, cells, tissues, microscopy images, or molecular structures are visible. The figure summarizes data categories only.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- No model architecture, embeddings, transformations, or computational pipeline are shown.\n\nFinding shown:\n- The majority of text sentences and images come from the pretraining category, with smaller contributions from biomedical/pathology, spatial omics, and general-domain sources."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 25, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 3: Cross-validation results for histology-based gene expression prediction. Supplementary performance of SciCore-Omics on the normal human heart spatial transcriptomics dataset using section-level 10-fold cross-validation. PCC and MSE were computed for the top 50 highly expressed genes on held-out sections under frozen-encoder and fine-tuned settings.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a two-panel grouped bar chart comparing model performance across 10 folds.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Top panel: titled “Fold-level MSE”; y-axis labeled “MSE”; x-axis shows Fold 1 through Fold 10.\n- Bottom panel: titled “Fold-level PCC”; y-axis labeled “PCC”; x-axis shows Fold 1 through Fold 10.\n\nLegend/model labels:\n- Loki\n- HisToGene\n- BLEEP\n- Hist2ST\n- Scicore-Omics(lp)\n- Scicore-Omics(ft)\n\nBiological source objects:\n- No explicit biological images, tissue samples, cells, genes, or microscopy objects are visible in this extracted figure.\n- The model names suggest spatial transcriptomics or histology-to-gene-expression related methods, but the figure itself only shows metric bars.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure appears to aggregate fold-level evaluation results into grouped bars.\n- Metrics shown are MSE and PCC for each method across folds.\n- No model architecture, input/output interface, or transformation pipeline is shown.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Scicore-Omics(lp) often has higher PCC bars than most other methods, especially in folds 5, 6, 8, and 9.\n- Scicore-Omics(ft) often has comparatively lower MSE in several folds, such as folds 1, 5, 7, 8, and 10.\n- Loki shows relatively high PCC in several folds but is not consistently best.\n- Performance varies substantially by fold for both MSE and PCC."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 26, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 4: UMAP visualization of pathway activity across SciCore-Omics embeddings. UMAP projections of image-only, gene-only and joint image-gene embeddings from the DLPFC spatial transcriptomics dataset, coloured by pathway activity scores for representative pathways, including Myelination , Oligodendrocyte differentiation , Axonogenesis and Neuron projection . Joint image-gene embeddings showed clearer pathway-associated organization than single-modality embeddings.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure showing UMAP scatter plots for biological embedding data. It contains 4 rows of biological terms and 3 columns of embedding spaces.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Columns are labeled:\n  - `image_emb`\n  - `gene_emb`\n  - `joint_emb`\n- Rows are labeled:\n  - `Myelination`\n  - `Oligodendrocyte_differentiation`\n  - `Axonogenesis`\n  - `Neuron_projection`\n\nAxes:\n- Each panel uses `UMAP-1` on the x-axis and `UMAP-2` on the y-axis.\n- Points are colored with a continuous colormap from dark purple/blue to green/yellow, likely indicating term-associated score or intensity, though no colorbar is visible.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- The labels refer to neurobiology-related gene ontology or biological process concepts:\n  - myelination\n  - oligodendrocyte differentiation\n  - axonogenesis\n  - neuron projection\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Three embedding modalities/interfaces are compared:\n  - image embeddings (`image_emb`)\n  - gene embeddings (`gene_emb`)\n  - joint embeddings (`joint_emb`)\n- Each embedding is visualized after dimensionality reduction using UMAP.\n\nVisible findings:\n- `gene_emb` panels show a large crescent/ring-like manifold with strong localized enrichment for myelination and oligodendrocyte differentiation near the upper-left region.\n- `Axonogenesis` and `Neuron_projection` show stronger signal over the right/lower-right regions of the `gene_emb` and `joint_emb` manifolds.\n- `image_emb` panels are more diffuse and less cleanly structured than `gene_emb` and `joint_emb`.\n- `joint_emb` preserves structured regions similar to `gene_emb` while showing separated manifold branches."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 27, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 5: Representative H&E-only case-level reasoning comparison. All models received the same H&E-only breast cancer case, while Ki-67 immunohistochemistry and pathology reports were withheld during inference. SciCore-Omics produced coherent evidence supporting a Ki-67-high conclusion; Qwen3-VL was relevant but verbose, whereas LLaVA-Med showed inconsistent reasoning and concluded Ki-67 low. Coloured dots indicate patch-level high, low or uncertain Ki-67 tendencies.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel comparative scientific figure about pathology/VLM interpretation of H&E histology images and Ki-67 assessment.\n\nVisible structure:\n- Left panel labeled “Shared H&E-only input”\n  - “Low-power overview”: whole-slide style low-magnification H&E tissue image.\n  - “Representative high-power”: H&E microscopy crop showing cellular tumor/stromal tissue with purple nuclei and pink extracellular/stromal areas.\n  - “Representative patch set”: four H&E patch thumbnails.\n  - Note box: “Ki-67 IHC and pathology report were withheld during inference and used only for expert review.”\n- Three model-output columns:\n  - “SciCore-Omics” marked “Coherent”\n  - “Qwen3-VL” marked “Verbose”\n  - “LLaVA-Med” marked “Contradictory”\n\nModel interface / evaluation design:\n- Each model column is organized into four numbered assessment sections:\n  1. Low-power interpretation\n  2. High-power evidence\n  3. Patch-level pattern\n  4. Case-level synthesis\n- Each section uses status icons: green check marks for SciCore-Omics, orange warning icons for Qwen3-VL, red cross icons for LLaVA-Med.\n- Patch-level Ki-67 pattern is visualized with rows of colored dots and legend:\n  - red/pink = High\n  - blue = Low\n  - yellow = Uncertain\n\nBiological source objects:\n- H&E-stained histopathology tissue sections.\n- Tumor-rich infiltrative regions, tumor-stroma interfaces, cellularity, nuclear atypia, hyperchromasia, suspected mitoses, necrosis/stromal context are referenced in the visible text.\n- Ki-67 immunohistochemistry is referenced as withheld ground-truth/expert-review information, not as visible input.\n\nFindings shown:\n- SciCore-Omics identifies tumor-rich infiltrative regions and tumor-stroma interfaces, cites cellularity, nuclear atypia, hyperchromasia, and suspected mitoses, reports a mostly consistent Ki-67-high tendency across regions, and concludes “Ki-67 high.”\n- Qwen3-VL is described as generally correct but over-interpreting necrosis, captures atypia/hyperchromasia and suspected mitoses, gives a mostly high but generic patch-level pattern, and concludes “Ki-67 high.”\n- LLaVA-Med mischaracterizes the lesion as benign fibroadenoma, gives template-like and inconsistent observations, mixes high/low/uncertain patch signals, and concludes “Ki-67 low” despite contradictory low-power interpretation."]}]

